       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sarcoidosis on corticosteroids:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " alendronate or another bisphosphonate may be the treatment of choice to prevent steroid-induced osteoporosis in at-risk patients (eg, older). Calcium/vitamin D supplements risk hypercalcemia (granulomas make active vitamin D): serum and 24-h urine calcium should be normal before starting them."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis (prednisone ≥ 7.5 mg/day equivalent with low BMD): 5 mg PO daily; 10 mg daily in postmenopausal women not on estrogen."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Not recommended if CrCl < 35 mL/min. Correct hypocalcemia (and vitamin D deficiency) before starting."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Calcium, antacids and other multivalent-cation drugs block absorption: wait at least one-half hour after alendronate before any other oral drug. Caution with aspirin/NSAIDs (GI irritation)."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
